1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,590
Taking a practice test
doesn't raise your SAT score.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:04,948
Reviewing it does.

3
00:00:04,968 --> 00:00:07,004
The test shows you what's broken.

4
00:00:07,147 --> 00:00:09,289
The review is where you fix it.

5
00:00:09,309 --> 00:00:11,646
Here's the mistake almost everyone makes.

6
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:14,468
They check the score, scroll
through the wrong answers,

7
00:00:14,488 --> 00:00:16,931
read a few explanations, think, okay,

8
00:00:16,951 --> 00:00:19,075
that makes sense, and take another test.

9
00:00:19,095 --> 00:00:21,809
Then they miss the same
kinds of questions again.

10
00:00:21,829 --> 00:00:24,701
In this video, you'll get a
step-by-step review method:

11
00:00:24,721 --> 00:00:28,258
how to sort every miss into
one of five categories,

12
00:00:28,329 --> 00:00:32,257
what goes in your mistake log,
how to find similar questions,

13
00:00:32,329 --> 00:00:35,757
and a weekly loop that
makes the fixes stick.

14
00:00:35,795 --> 00:00:39,347
A full SAT is two hours and
fourteen minutes of testing:

15
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:42,989
sixty-four minutes of Reading and
Writing and seventy minutes of math.

16
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:45,116
That's ninety-eight questions.

17
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:47,560
Every question you miss is information.

18
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:51,382
It points to a rule you don't
know, a habit that's costing you,

19
00:00:51,402 --> 00:00:53,418
or a spot where you run out of time.

20
00:00:53,438 --> 00:00:56,399
If you don't look closely, you
throw that information away.

21
00:00:56,419 --> 00:00:58,256
There's also a supply problem.

22
00:00:58,276 --> 00:01:02,613
College Board currently lists eight
full-length SAT practice tests.

23
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:04,756
If you burn through
them without reviewing,

24
00:01:04,776 --> 00:01:07,882
you run out of official tests
before you run out of mistakes.

25
00:01:07,957 --> 00:01:11,151
College Board's own study
advice says the same thing.

26
00:01:11,171 --> 00:01:13,223
Instead of just checking
the right answers,

27
00:01:13,243 --> 00:01:15,579
figure out why you missed each question,

28
00:01:15,599 --> 00:01:17,365
take notes, and look for patterns.

29
00:01:17,385 --> 00:01:18,473
That's the whole idea.

30
00:01:18,493 --> 00:01:19,850
Let's make it concrete.

31
00:01:19,925 --> 00:01:22,369
After you finish a
practice test in Bluebook,

32
00:01:22,389 --> 00:01:24,533
your results go to My Practice.

33
00:01:24,568 --> 00:01:27,389
Log in with the same account
you used in Bluebook.

34
00:01:27,419 --> 00:01:31,721
Open Score Details, and you get a
list of every question on the test,

35
00:01:31,741 --> 00:01:33,899
with the correct answer,
the answer you picked,

36
00:01:33,919 --> 00:01:37,273
and a Review button that
breaks down each answer choice.

37
00:01:37,293 --> 00:01:39,666
You can also download a
practice score report.

38
00:01:39,686 --> 00:01:41,988
It shows how many questions you got right,

39
00:01:42,008 --> 00:01:44,987
and your performance
in each content domain,

40
00:01:45,007 --> 00:01:47,604
as a bar of up to seven boxes.

41
00:01:47,624 --> 00:01:49,032
And there are two shortcuts.

42
00:01:49,052 --> 00:01:53,104
Get Tailored Practice builds a set of
Question Bank questions based on your

43
00:01:53,124 --> 00:01:53,854
results.

44
00:01:53,874 --> 00:01:56,354
And for every official practice test,

45
00:01:56,374 --> 00:02:00,640
Khan Academy has a walk-through course
that explains each question and links to

46
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:02,267
lessons on the skills.

47
00:02:02,312 --> 00:02:03,740
All of that is useful.

48
00:02:03,776 --> 00:02:07,756
But if you only read explanations,
you'll understand them in the moment and

49
00:02:07,776 --> 00:02:09,312
still miss the next one.

50
00:02:09,597 --> 00:02:12,336
So here's the order to do things in.

51
00:02:12,356 --> 00:02:15,320
The first step happens
during the test itself.

52
00:02:15,463 --> 00:02:18,871
On your scratch paper, keep a list
of every question you guessed on,

53
00:02:18,891 --> 00:02:20,407
even the fifty-fifty ones.

54
00:02:20,427 --> 00:02:22,371
Write the module and question number.

55
00:02:22,391 --> 00:02:23,523
You'll need it later.

56
00:02:23,543 --> 00:02:26,615
Then, before you read
a single explanation,

57
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:30,595
open every question you missed
and every one you guessed on,

58
00:02:30,615 --> 00:02:32,522
and redo it cold.

59
00:02:32,542 --> 00:02:33,558
No timer.

60
00:02:33,578 --> 00:02:34,559
No hints.

61
00:02:34,579 --> 00:02:36,659
Just you and the question.

62
00:02:36,679 --> 00:02:40,731
This is the most important step, because
it tells you what kind of miss it was.

63
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:43,052
If you get it right with no time pressure,

64
00:02:43,072 --> 00:02:46,143
you knew the material, and
something else went wrong.

65
00:02:46,178 --> 00:02:49,393
If you still can't do it,
you've found a real content gap.

66
00:02:49,426 --> 00:02:52,405
Only after the redo, read the explanation,

67
00:02:52,425 --> 00:02:55,462
including why each wrong answer is wrong.

68
00:02:55,819 --> 00:02:59,441
Wrong answers on the SAT usually
look tempting for a reason,

69
00:02:59,461 --> 00:03:03,462
and knowing why you liked one matters
as much as knowing the right one.

70
00:03:03,514 --> 00:03:06,458
Now sort every miss into
one of five categories.

71
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:09,709
If you used the four labels
from the last-week video,

72
00:03:09,729 --> 00:03:11,851
this is the same idea with more detail.

73
00:03:11,871 --> 00:03:15,209
A trap miss, where you answered
something the question didn't ask,

74
00:03:15,229 --> 00:03:17,243
counts as a misread here.

75
00:03:17,263 --> 00:03:19,215
And guessed right is new.

76
00:03:19,235 --> 00:03:21,179
One, content gap.

77
00:03:21,199 --> 00:03:24,393
You couldn't solve it even untimed,
because you don't know the rule,

78
00:03:24,413 --> 00:03:26,128
formula, or skill.

79
00:03:26,163 --> 00:03:31,162
The fix is to learn it: a lesson,
then practice on that skill.

80
00:03:31,194 --> 00:03:33,444
Two, misread.

81
00:03:33,552 --> 00:03:36,032
You solved a different question
than the one they asked.

82
00:03:36,052 --> 00:03:39,158
You found x when they asked for two x.

83
00:03:39,266 --> 00:03:41,230
You missed the word not.

84
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:45,336
The fix is a habit: reread the
question line before you pick.

85
00:03:45,416 --> 00:03:47,238
Three, careless.

86
00:03:47,345 --> 00:03:49,075
You read it right and knew how to do it,

87
00:03:49,095 --> 00:03:52,289
but the execution slipped: a sign error,

88
00:03:52,309 --> 00:03:55,202
an arithmetic mistake, a
click on the wrong letter.

89
00:03:55,274 --> 00:03:57,896
The fix is to write out
the step that slipped,

90
00:03:57,916 --> 00:04:00,024
and check with Desmos when that's quick.

91
00:04:00,087 --> 00:04:01,837
Four, timing.

92
00:04:01,944 --> 00:04:04,317
You rushed it or never really got to it,

93
00:04:04,337 --> 00:04:06,602
and untimed, you get it right.

94
00:04:06,622 --> 00:04:10,387
The fix is pacing: two
passes, and mark and move,

95
00:04:10,407 --> 00:04:11,792
from the pacing video.

96
00:04:11,812 --> 00:04:14,097
And five, guessed right.

97
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:16,098
You got the point, but you don't own it.

98
00:04:16,134 --> 00:04:17,526
Treat it like a miss.

99
00:04:17,598 --> 00:04:20,062
Usually, it's a content gap in disguise.

100
00:04:20,086 --> 00:04:23,173
The tricky pair is
misread versus careless.

101
00:04:23,193 --> 00:04:26,530
Ask yourself: did I
answer the right question?

102
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:28,550
If not, it's a misread.

103
00:04:28,586 --> 00:04:32,408
If yes, but I messed up
the work, it's careless.

104
00:04:32,441 --> 00:04:33,441
Try one.

105
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:36,278
A question asks for the value of two x.

106
00:04:36,298 --> 00:04:40,134
A student solves correctly,
gets x equals six,

107
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:41,278
and picks six.

108
00:04:41,298 --> 00:04:42,868
Which category is that?

109
00:04:42,924 --> 00:04:44,138
Pause and try it.

110
00:04:47,349 --> 00:04:48,294
Misread.

111
00:04:48,314 --> 00:04:50,972
The math was right, but they
answered a different question.

112
00:04:50,992 --> 00:04:53,329
So the fix isn't more algebra practice.

113
00:04:53,349 --> 00:04:57,079
It's rereading what the question
asks for before choosing.

114
00:04:57,099 --> 00:04:59,135
The answer they needed was twelve.

115
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:03,374
Every miss goes in your mistake log,
the same error log from the study plans

116
00:05:03,394 --> 00:05:04,394
video.

117
00:05:04,787 --> 00:05:06,073
A spreadsheet works.

118
00:05:06,286 --> 00:05:07,195
So does a notebook.

119
00:05:07,215 --> 00:05:08,608
Here are the columns.

120
00:05:08,628 --> 00:05:10,679
Where it's from: the test, the module,

121
00:05:10,699 --> 00:05:12,215
and the question number.

122
00:05:12,235 --> 00:05:16,073
Then the section, domain, and
skill, using College Board's names,

123
00:05:16,093 --> 00:05:18,271
so you can find them in
the Question Bank later.

124
00:05:18,298 --> 00:05:20,243
Then your answer, the correct answer,

125
00:05:20,263 --> 00:05:23,350
and the category: content gap, misread,

126
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:25,912
careless, timing, or guessed right.

127
00:05:25,932 --> 00:05:27,911
Then the two columns that matter most.

128
00:05:27,931 --> 00:05:32,591
Why: one sentence about the
exact moment it went wrong.

129
00:05:32,611 --> 00:05:34,055
Not, I didn't get it.

130
00:05:34,075 --> 00:05:36,948
Something like: I picked
a choice that was true,

131
00:05:36,968 --> 00:05:38,980
but it didn't answer the question.

132
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,586
And the fix, written as
a rule for next time.

133
00:05:41,606 --> 00:05:45,658
For example: on circle equations,
the right side is r squared,

134
00:05:45,678 --> 00:05:46,678
not r.

135
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:50,821
Or: when two choices are both true,
pick the one that answers the question.

136
00:05:50,849 --> 00:05:55,043
Last, two redo columns, each with a
date and whether you got it right.

137
00:05:55,063 --> 00:05:56,396
We'll use those in a minute.

138
00:05:56,416 --> 00:05:58,682
One missed question is one example.

139
00:05:58,702 --> 00:06:01,861
To fix the skill, you need
more questions like it.

140
00:06:01,881 --> 00:06:05,119
That's what the free SAT
Suite Question Bank is for.

141
00:06:05,139 --> 00:06:08,976
Filter by section, domain, and
skill to match the log entry.

142
00:06:08,996 --> 00:06:12,512
For difficulty, College Board
suggests using your score report.

143
00:06:12,532 --> 00:06:15,296
If that domain has one
to three boxes filled,

144
00:06:15,316 --> 00:06:16,512
start with easy.

145
00:06:16,532 --> 00:06:18,547
Four or five, medium.

146
00:06:18,567 --> 00:06:20,241
Six or seven, hard.

147
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:22,991
Pick about ten, export
them without answers,

148
00:06:23,011 --> 00:06:25,062
and do them as a mini quiz.

149
00:06:25,082 --> 00:06:28,704
Then export them again with
answers and explanations to check.

150
00:06:28,724 --> 00:06:31,332
Any new misses go straight into the log.

151
00:06:31,412 --> 00:06:33,749
Or use Get Tailored
Practice in My Practice,

152
00:06:33,769 --> 00:06:35,821
which builds a set for you.

153
00:06:35,841 --> 00:06:39,928
Either way, you're practicing the exact
skill the test said you're missing.

154
00:06:39,948 --> 00:06:43,857
Understanding an explanation once
isn't the same as being able to do the

155
00:06:43,877 --> 00:06:44,571
question.

156
00:06:44,591 --> 00:06:48,013
So every log entry gets
redone cold, later.

157
00:06:48,033 --> 00:06:51,156
Redo it about three days later,
without looking at your notes.

158
00:06:51,176 --> 00:06:52,549
Then again about a week after that.

159
00:06:52,569 --> 00:06:55,191
If you get it right both times, retire it.

160
00:06:55,211 --> 00:06:58,977
If you miss it, it stays in the log,
and you go back to the lesson for that

161
00:06:58,997 --> 00:06:59,635
skill.

162
00:06:59,655 --> 00:07:03,564
This is the step people skip, and
it's the one that makes fixes stick.

163
00:07:03,584 --> 00:07:07,492
A question you could explain right after
reading the answer can fall apart a

164
00:07:07,512 --> 00:07:08,421
week later.

165
00:07:08,441 --> 00:07:10,941
The redo shows you which ones.

166
00:07:10,966 --> 00:07:14,179
Now put it all together
into a weekly loop.

167
00:07:14,232 --> 00:07:18,197
On test day, or the day
after, review the test.

168
00:07:18,482 --> 00:07:21,947
Redo every miss and every guess cold,

169
00:07:22,161 --> 00:07:24,803
read the explanations,
and fill in the log.

170
00:07:24,874 --> 00:07:28,604
On weekdays, spend ten to
fifteen minutes on log redos,

171
00:07:28,624 --> 00:07:31,160
plus a Question Bank
set on your top skill.

172
00:07:31,201 --> 00:07:33,287
At the end of the week,
count your categories.

173
00:07:33,307 --> 00:07:36,236
Whatever shows up most decides next week.

174
00:07:36,486 --> 00:07:37,716
Mostly content gaps?

175
00:07:37,736 --> 00:07:39,715
More lessons and skill practice.

176
00:07:39,735 --> 00:07:41,271
Mostly misreads?

177
00:07:41,378 --> 00:07:43,038
Slow down on the question line.

178
00:07:43,058 --> 00:07:44,037
Careless?

179
00:07:44,057 --> 00:07:45,144
Write more steps.

180
00:07:45,164 --> 00:07:45,859
Timing?

181
00:07:45,879 --> 00:07:47,129
Pacing drills.

182
00:07:47,165 --> 00:07:48,860
And watch the counts over time.

183
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:53,003
If they shift from content gaps
toward careless and timing,

184
00:07:53,023 --> 00:07:53,753
that's a good sign.

185
00:07:53,773 --> 00:07:55,431
It means you're learning the material,

186
00:07:55,451 --> 00:07:58,200
and it's time to sharpen how you execute.

187
00:07:58,240 --> 00:07:59,041
Quick recap.

188
00:07:59,061 --> 00:08:02,399
Redo every miss cold before
reading the explanation.

189
00:08:02,419 --> 00:08:04,863
Sort it into one of five categories.

190
00:08:04,883 --> 00:08:07,006
Log it with a why and a fix.

191
00:08:07,026 --> 00:08:09,327
Find similar questions
in the Question Bank.

192
00:08:09,347 --> 00:08:12,256
Redo it a few days later,
and again a week later.

193
00:08:12,276 --> 00:08:15,280
Then let the counts decide
what you study next.

194
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:16,871
If this helped, subscribe.

195
00:08:17,335 --> 00:08:19,673
The next video puts everything to work:

196
00:08:19,693 --> 00:08:24,549
a full SAT math module, walked
through question by question.
